From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,rec.arts.poems
Subject:  Re: Northwestern U. computer policies
Date: 7 May 1994 15:51:36 -0400
Message-ID: <2qgrg8$5vc@eff.org>

Summary: This is a critique of the policies of Northwestern U.'s
Academic Computing and Network Services (ACNS) organization. The
intellectual freedom policy is very good. Due-process policy is
fatally flawed: ACNS improperly claims authority to judge alleged
offensives and impose summary punishments on students. This
contradicts explicit University policy (and the principles of academic
freedom). The privacy policy is better than most, but it is
confusingly expressed.  Northwestern U. general policy also requires
that students be given a voice in creating policy that affects them. I
don't know how (or if) students participated in the creation of these
policies.

Aside: Northwestern U. has one of the best general student policies
I've ever read. Even better, most all its polices are on-line.
    [See gopher -p"1/handbook/happend" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70]


============
>ACNS Guidelines for the Use of Network and Computing 
>Resources at Northwestern University
[...]

[see gopher -p"1/computers/ACNS Information/acns" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70]

________________________
>* Privacy. [...]
>if a user is suspected of violations of the responsibilities as
>stated in this document, their right to privacy is superseded by the
>University's requirement to maintain the network's integrity and the
>rights of all network users.

This is vague. It suggests that a system operator could decide on his
or her own to search a user's files. It is clarified later:

>Should the security of a computer system be threatened, user files
>may be examined under the direction of the Director or Associate
>Director of ACNS, the Director of UMS, the Director of UCC, or the
>Vice President of Information Systems and Technology.

Which seems more in line with the university's general privacy policy:

>     7. The University will not act in derogation of the 
> rights of students to be secure in their possessions. 
> Students will be secure against invasion of privacy and 
> unreasonable search and seizure.

I suggest removing the first, vague, statement.

________________________
>* Safety.

>While harassment or unwanted unsolicited contact cannot be controlled
>on the network, network users who receive threatening communications
>should bring them to the attention of ACNS.

No, they shouldn't. This advice contradicts official University policy
on sexual harassment.

ACNS is *not* authorized to handle sexual harassment complaints.
Protected expression is often separated from unprotected expression
only by a dim and uncertain line. In order to find this line,
Northwestern U. has a "University Policy on Sexual Harassment".  It
says:

    A member of the University community who believes that he or she
    has been the victim of sexual harassment or who becomes aware of
    an incident of sexual harassment as defined above should bring any
    such matter to the attention of either the school dean, the
    assistant vice president for student affairs, or the associate
    vice president for human resources, as he or she prefers.
    [See gopher -p"1/handbook/happend" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70]

For other types of alleged harassment, I believe official University
procedure is to file a complaint with the office of the executive
secretary of the University Hearing & Appeals Board. (This office
also works with this less formal Conciliation Board).
    [See gopher -p"1/handbook/happend" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70
       Select "Filing of Complaints"]

________________________
>* Intellectual Freedom.

>The network is a free and open forum for the expression, including
>viewpoints that are strange, unorthodox, or unpopular. The network
>administrators place no official sanctions upon the expression of
>personal opinion on the network. As stated in the Policy Statement on
>Student Rights and Responsibilities, students are free from
>censorship in expressing their views, as long as their views are not
>represented as the views of Northwestern University.

Excellent.

________________________
[...]
>If you knowingly violate a network responsibility, your network
>access will be suspended. Depending on the seriousness of the
>violation, you could be referred through the University disciplinary
>procedure process.

This suggests that computer access can be suspended without due
process. Not only is this a violation of academic freedom, it is a
violation of Northwestern's "University Regulations: Computing and
Telecommunications Equipment, Facilities, and Services" which
authorizes no summary computer expulsion, instead it says:

   Students who violate these or any other computing or
   telecommunications facilities regulations shall be subject to
   University disciplinary procedures that may include fines,
   restitution of funds, probation, suspension, or expulsion from the
   University.
     gopher -p"1/handbook/handbook/studcond" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70
     then select: "Policy Statement on Student Rights & Responsibilities"

The ACNS rule also violates the jurisdiction of the University Hearing
and Appeals System. According to student code:

    The University Hearing and Appeals System shall have jurisdiction
   over all cases, other than those arising because of unsatisfactory
   academic work, which may call for discipline of a student, group of
   students, or student organization (undergraduate or graduate) of
   any school on the Evanston campus, including cases concerning the
   rights or property of the University or of the members of the
   University community and the rights and property of any person if
   the acts complained of occurred on premises under the control of
   the University.

The University does have a policy on summary suspensions from campus).
It says that summary suspensions can be authorized only by the
president, a designated vice president, or by the dean of the Chicago
campus. And only for reasons relating to the safety and well-being of
students, faculty, or University property.
     [gopher -p"1/handbook/happend" nuinfo.nwu.edu 70]


________________________
[...]
>4. You must not deliberately perform an act which will 
>seriously impact the operation of computers, terminals, 
>peripherals, or networks.

I suggest changing "impact" to "disrupt", since even proper use of a
computer or terminal can "seriously impact" its operation. ("Disrupt"
is also the word used in "Trustee Statement on Disruption".)

________________________
>5. You must not [...] give to another [] a program which could result in 
>the eventual damage to a file or computer system and/or the 
>reproduction of itself.
[...]

This is so broad that I think it violates the University's commitment
to free expression. It make you responsible for the possible actions
of another. Giving a program to another person is expression and
should be protected as far as possible. Almost any program *could*
result in damage depending on what that other person does with it.

I suggest something along the lines of the _Brandenburg v.  Ohio_
Supreme Court decision which said that expression may not be
suppressed or punished unless it is intended to produce 'imminent
lawless action' and it is 'likely to produce such action.'

________________________
>6. You must not attempt to [...] uncover security loopholes.

and later

>ACNS should be notified  [...] about potential loopholes in the 
>security of its computer systems and networks.

These rules contradict each other. I suggest dropping the first
one.

________________________
[...]
>you must not 
>make copies of copyrighted software, unless ACNS has a site 
>license specifically allowing the copying of that software. 

This needs to be worded better. As it stands, it outlaws use of GNU
software. I suggest something like:

   You must not copy software in violation of copyright or
   license.

________________________
[...]
>10. You must not attempt to monitor another user's data 
>communications, nor may you read, copy, change, or delete 
>another user's files or software, without permission of the 
>owner.
[...]

The word "owner" should be changed to "user" to allow for monitoring
with permission

________________________
[...]
>11. You must not use any of ACNS' microcomputers, 
>workstations, or networks for other than a Northwestern 
>University course, research project, departmental activity, 
>or personal communications. These resources must not be used 
>for personal or financial gain.

This seems to prohibit self-directed study (typically done for
personal, nonfinancial gain).

________________________
Another ACNS statement:

>============================================
>Source: ACNS
>Last updated: June 15, 1993
>
>PRIVACY ISSUES
>
>Just as there's nothing stopping the phone company from 
>listening in on your conversations,

The law prohibits the phone company of arbitrary listening. Also, when
phone companies to need to listen to a line to check quality, it is
the policy of most to use a voice scrambler so that conversations are
unintelligible.

>people with system privileges have the power to examine all the mail
>that passes through "their" systems.

I suggest replacing the broad "power" with "technical ability".

[...]
>ACNS reserves the right to take whatever steps are necessary 
>to investigate security threats or violations of ACNS 
>guidelines or University regulations.

So the ACNS reserves the right to commit murder? I suggest replacing
"whatever steps" with "reasonable steps".

This whole "Privacy Issues" statement seems redundant and confusing. I
suggest killing it. The privacy section on the ACNS's Guidelines
already covers this area.

________________________

Finally, with respect to user participation in policy making, the
University's "Policy Statement on Student Rights and Responsibilities"
says:

     14. Students will have their views and welfare 
    considered in the formation of University policy and will be 
    consulted by or represented on University committees that 
    affect students as members of the University community.

I don't know how (or even if) students and other users participated in
the creation of these ACNS policies.


