gvrod@isuvax.iastate.edu (Rod Eldridge) writes:

>Recent postings to most of the soc.culture.* newsgroups by Andrzej Janus
>(ajanus@iastate.edu) offered information about an immigrant lottery
>program.  Upon requesting the information, it was offered for a fee. 
>This for-profit activity violates Iowa State University policies (not to
>mention Usenet News rules) resulting in the current suspension of
>Andrzej's login name.  We regret any inconvenience these activities may
>have caused the Usenet News community.  The postings have been canceled.

I don't understand: How could Mr. Janus' punishment be legally
determined so quickly? How can his punishment be legally announced
publicly?

Along with forbidding commercial postings, Iowa State University's
Computer Ethics policy has an excellent due process section. It says:

    Violations of the University Code of Computer Ethics are treated
    like any other ethical violation as outlined in the Student
    Handbook and applicable faculty and staff handbooks.

[Via gopher, try:
 gopher -p"1/departments/Computation Center/Online Documentation" info.iastate.edu 70
then select: "ISU Code of Computer Ethics"]

I'm very surprised the University apparently was able to provide the
due process that it promises (and that the Constitution requires) so
quickly.

Also, the University's public announcement seems to violate federal
law. The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act forbids public
announcements of student punishments (unless the the student waives
his rights).

So, either
  1. The student waived his right to a formal hearing and his right to
     privacy and agreed to be suspended from the computer and to have
     the suspension made public.
Or,
  
  2. Iowa State U. reacted to one apparent violation of University
     policy with by violating its own policy, a federal law, and
     constitutional protections of due process.

If anyone at Iowa State U. who hasn't had their email taken away yet
knows Andrzej Janus, please give him a copy of this message.

- Carl

