No idea, but I don't think so.   (That is, apart from alt.sex which
has never really been deliberately imported into Aust, except for a
shortish period via an alternate link which I think has stopped now).

| If so, can anyone give an explanation for why these groups
| have stopped being propagated ?

Here's my guess ... apparently (from your message) you aren't getting
those groups from harbinger, that leaves just munnari.   Munnari is slow
and newshost.anu.edu.au is as slow or slower.  Number one effect is that
munnari is tending to be (at least) several days behind on news, though
that wouldn't be affecting anything.  Number two effect is that munnari
tends to have a pretty large queue of articles to be sent to ANU (and one
or two other sites).  Combine that with lack of disc space on munnari,
meaning that some newsgroups are expired very quickly (I think I have
alt.binaries set at about 4 hours), and you get cases where all of the
articles are expired before munnari has a chance to send them.

ie: if you're relying on munnari and your news server is a bit sluggish,
then you're probably going to miss a lot.

The relationship with the introduction of the feed from harbinger to ANU
is probably (a guess) that that feed increased the load at ANU, leaving
less cycles available for the feed from munnari, slowing it even more than
it was before.

At least that has been what's happened up to today - there is getting to
be a better & better chance that Melb Uni may in the not too distant future
acquire a better news server which should be able to keep up with the
world a little better.

However, none of that will affect any of the groups you're asking about,
as, as of last week, the university has decided that it will no longer
have anything to do with any of the groups that you were asking about.
Right now nothing has yet been done to implement that policy, but when
I stop typing this message it will be....

Don't send me messages about this, it won't help, instead address paper
mail to "Vice Chancellor, University of Melbourne, Parkville, 3052" and
refer to the policy instituted by the Registrar of Information Technology
(if you feel the need that is).

Sending messages in newsgroups won't help either.

